# .blitzyignore
# Assumes gitignore-style pattern syntax. Verify against Blitzy's docs if behavior differs.
#
# Scope: ONLY files nothing can read, plus the one directory that would give the
# demo away. Everything with extractable content stays visible, including images,
# PDFs and Office documents. Images in particular are valuable here:
# legacy/screenshots/ is the only surviving record of the original app running,
# since the hosted demo is gone.

# --- The migrated target ---
# This is the single exception to the "unreadable binaries only" rule above, and it
# is not about readability. migrated/ is a hand-written .NET build of the same app,
# produced from the extracted XML. It is readable, and it is useful to humans, but
# to an ingestion run it is the answer key: if Blitzy reads the finished target
# alongside the legacy input, it is transcribing rather than modernizing, and the
# demo proves nothing.
#
# Remove this rule only if the intent is to let Blitzy see the target, for example
# to score its own output against a reference.
migrated/

# --- OutSystems binaries ---
# Verified opaque: a header plus compressed binary XML fragments, no readable text.
# The extracted XML in legacy-oml-xml/ is what carries this content.
*.oml
*.oap
*.osp
*.osl
*.oss

# --- Compiled output ---
*.dll
*.exe
*.pdb
*.so
*.dylib
*.class
*.o
*.obj

# --- Archives ---
# Opaque until extracted. Extract first, then let the contents be ingested.
*.zip
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
*.7z
*.rar
*.jar
*.war
*.nupkg

# --- Database binaries and backups ---
*.bak
*.mdf
*.ldf
*.dmp
*.sqlite
*.db

# --- Video ---
*.mp4
*.mov
*.avi

# --- Fonts ---
*.ttf
*.otf
*.woff
*.woff2
*.eot

# --- OS binary metadata ---
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db